  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nCurrent conditions/Today:\n\nSurface high pressure is centered over southern MN this morning,\nwith light or calm winds across the Northland, with temperatures\ncurrently in the single digits and teens, with the coldest\nlocations along the Iron Range and over the Arrowhead. Expect an\nincrease in high clouds through the morning, but do not expect\nmuch impact from those. \n\nSurface high pressure will slide to the east today, toward the\neastern Great Lakes, as an upper level low advances southeast \nout of the Canadian Prairies and into the Central Plains. The \nupper low lacks moisture, and only an increase in cloud cover is\nexpected, with no precipitation. Low level winds will swing \naround to the southwest as the coldest low level temps shift \neastward. Temperatures will warm into the 30s for most of the \narea today, with a few upper 20s over the tip of the Arrowhead. \nWith light southwest breezes, wind chills will remain in the mid\n20s for most locations through the day. Temperatures tonight \nwill fall into the teens again tonight.\n\nRest of the work week:\n\nQuiet weather will remain in play through the work week and into \nThanksgiving. Temperatures will gradually be on increasing \nthrough the week with highs on Thanksgiving and Friday in the \nupper 30s to low 40s. Overnight temperatures will consistently \nbe below freezing.\n\nThe Weekend:\n\nOur next round of precipitation could arrive this weekend. The 12Z \nsuite of guidance is highlighting another low pressure moving across \nManitoba into Ontario. Most ensemble members keep this system north \nof the International Border with the latest AI models also inline \nwith this parameter spacing. However, run to run consistency remains \nlow. The current NBM is maintaining some low end PoPs of 20-40% \nspilling over into the region with the highest chances along the \nInternational Border. So for the time being additional snow \naccumulations remain very low through the weekend.",
